# @superinstance/polyvocoder (TypeScript Binding)

> **TypeScript client for the Polyvocoder universal head.**
> Schema-parity with the Python polyvocoder. Built for the Quilt substrate walker
> polyformalism fleet.

## What is this?

This is the TypeScript client for [SuperInstance/polyvocoder](https://github.com/SuperInstance/polyvocoder).
The Python polyvocoder exposes a REST API; this TypeScript package wraps that API
with full type safety.

The Python and TypeScript implementations share an identical JSON schema (see
`src/types/index.ts`). Both are pinned to the Quilt fleet canary hash
`0x024a555471370b18d` for byte-exact reproducibility.

## Quick Start

```typescript
import { PolyvocoderClient } from "@superinstance/polyvocoder";

const client = new PolyvocoderClient();

const result = await client.runPipeline({
  text: "The canon gate made itself heard.",
  nSamples: 3,
});

console.log("JEV features:", result.features);
for (const sample of result.decodedSamples) {
  console.log("---");
  console.log("Text:", sample.text);
  console.log("Image:");
  console.log(sample.image);
  console.log("Audio samples:", sample.audio.length);
}
```

## Schema Parity

The TypeScript types mirror the Python dataclasses exactly:

```typescript
// src/types/index.ts
export interface JEVFeatures {
  canonWorthy: number;
  distinctVoice: number;
  doctrineAnchor: number;
  voiceFit: number;
  novelty: number;
  density: number;
}

export interface DecodedSample {
  latent: number[];
  text: string;
  image: string;
  audio: Float32Array | number[];
}

export interface PipelineResult {
  features: JEVFeatures;
  decodedSamples: DecodedSample[];
}
```

The Python side uses snake_case (`canon_worthy`); TypeScript uses camelCase
(`canonWorthy`). The JSON wire format includes both spellings for compatibility.

