# Deploying the Polyvocoder Backend

The TypeScript binding is a thin HTTP client. It needs a Python backend to talk to.
This document explains how to deploy the Python polyvocoder as an HTTP service.

## Option 1: Local Development

```bash
# Clone the Python polyvocoder
git clone https://github.com/SuperInstance/polyvocoder
cd polyvocoder
pip install --break-system-packages -e .

# Run the Python service (simple HTTP server)
python3 -m polyvocoder.serve --port 8000
```

Then point the TypeScript client at it:

```typescript
const client = new PolyvocoderClient("http://localhost:8000");
```

## Option 2: Cloudflare Worker + Python Service

Deploy the TypeScript binding as a Cloudflare Worker that proxies to a Python
service:

```typescript
// worker.ts
export default {
  async fetch(request: Request, env: Env): Promise<Response> {
    const client = new PolyvocoderClient(env.POLYVOCODER_URL);
    const result = await client.runPipeline({ text: await request.text() });
    return new Response(JSON.stringify(result));
  },
};
```

The Python backend can run anywhere (Fly.io, Railway, a VPS, etc.).

## Option 3: Serverless GPU

For higher throughput, deploy the Python polyvocoder on:
- AWS Lambda (cold start ~3-5s, fine for canonical lore generation)
- Google Cloud Run (auto-scaling)
- Fly.io (always-on, low cost)
- Railway (simple deploy from GitHub)

The polyvocoder has no GPU dependency and runs in <500MB RAM.

## Option 4: Self-hosted on Your Own Server

```bash
# On your server
git clone https://github.com/SuperInstance/polyvocoder
cd polyvocoder
pip install --break-system-packages -e .
gunicorn -w 4 -b 0.0.0.0:8000 polyvocoder.serve:app
```

Then point your TypeScript client at it:
```typescript
const client = new PolyvocoderClient("https://polyvocoder.your-domain.com");
```

## Required Environment Variables

The Python backend needs:
- `TYPESAFEAI_KEY` — JEV API key (https://typesafe.ai)

The TypeScript binding needs:
- The endpoint URL (passed to the constructor)

## Scaling Considerations

- **JEV API rate limits**: ~5 QPS on the Lite tier. For higher throughput,
  upgrade your Typesafe.ai plan.
- **VAE training is fast**: <1 second per lore.
- **Head rendering is fast**: <0.1 second per sample.
- **Total latency**: 5-30 seconds per lore (dominated by JEV).

For high-throughput use cases, batch lore generation client-side and send
multiple lores to the backend in parallel.

## Authentication

For production deployments, add authentication:

```typescript
// TypeScript side
class PolyvocoderClient {
  constructor(
    private endpoint: string,
    private apiKey?: string,
  ) {}

  async runPipeline(lore: LoreInput): Promise<PipelineResult> {
    const headers: Record<string, string> = { "Content-Type": "application/json" };
    if (this.apiKey) headers["Authorization"] = `Bearer ${this.apiKey}`;

    const response = await fetch(`${this.endpoint}/v1/pipeline`, {
      method: "POST",
      headers,
      body: JSON.stringify(lore),
    });
    // ...
  }
}
```

## Monitoring

Add logging and metrics:

```typescript
async runPipeline(lore: LoreInput): Promise<PipelineResult> {
  const start = Date.now();
  try {
    const result = await this.runPipelineInternal(lore);
    console.log(`Polyvocoder: ${Date.now() - start}ms, ${lore.text.length} chars`);
    return result;
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(`Polyvocoder failed: ${e.message}`);
    throw e;
  }
}
```

## Cost Estimation

Per 1000 lore generations:
- JEV API: 1000 × $0.04 / 1M tokens × ~300 tokens = $0.012
- Python backend: 1000 × 5s × $0.000016/s = $0.08 (on cheap VPS)
- TypeScript binding: free (no compute)
- Total: ~$0.10 per 1000 lores

For 10,000 lore generations per day: ~$1/day.

